{"name":"Camel Cord","hex":"#e0cb82","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"camel","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mammalian beast of burden, much used in desert areas, of the genus Camelus.","A light brownish color, like that of a camel (also called camel brown)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a light brown color like that of a camel."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/camel"},{"term":"cord","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A long, thin, flexible length of twisted yarns (strands) of fibre (a rope, for example).","Any quantity of such material when viewed as a mass or commodity."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To furnish with cords.","To tie or fasten with cords."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cord"}],"entries":[{"title":"Camel","blocks":[{"text":"A camel is an even-toed ungulate in the genus Camelus that bears distinctive fatty deposits known as \"humps\" on its back. Camels have long been domesticated and, as livestock, they provide food and textiles. Camels are working animals especially suited to their desert habitat and are a vital means of transport for passengers and cargo. There are three surviving species of camel. The one-humped dromedary makes up 94% of the world's camel population, and the two-humped Bactrian camel makes up 6%. The wild Bactrian camel is a distinct species that is not ancestral to the domestic Bactrian camel, and is now endangered, with around 1,000 individuals and a continuing decline."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/07._Camel_Profile%2C_near_Silverton%2C_NSW%2C_07.07.2007.jpg/330px-07._Camel_Profile%2C_near_Silverton%2C_NSW%2C_07.07.2007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camel","wikidata":"Q7375"},{"title":"Camel (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Camel is a color that resembles the color of the hair of a camel."}],"image":"https://thumb